(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-inflammation-quiz"]={"title":"Inflammation — Quiz 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the three linked vascular mechanisms the lecture describes?","choices":["Endothelial contraction, then exudation, then vasodilation of arterioles","Exudation, then vasodilation, then endothelial contraction","Vasodilation of arterioles, then endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability, then exudation","Endothelial contraction, then vasodilation of the arterioles, then leukocyte transmigration into the tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation raises blood flow, endothelial contraction opens the capillaries, and fluid and blood proteins then move into the interstitial spaces. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which two of the listed subsets both act against virus-infected and tumour cells?","choices":["T helper cells and regulatory suppressor T cells","B lymphocytes and plasma cells","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells","Monocytes and macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives both the same target — virus-infected and tumour cells. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which vascular event immediately follows increased vascular permeability in the reviewed sequence?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the arterioles","Haemoconcentration and stasis","Chemotaxis","Phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As fluid and protein leave the vessel, the remaining blood concentrates and flow slows — which is what allows leukocytes to marginate and adhere next. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why does stasis of blood flow matter mechanistically in acute inflammation?","choices":["It marks the point at which acute inflammation gives way to chronic inflammation in the tissue","It reverses the earlier increase in permeability","It terminates the inflammatory response","It precedes leukocyte adhesion in the sequence, as slowed flow allows leukocytes to contact the endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Haemoconcentration and stasis sit immediately before leukocyte adhesion in the reviewed sequence. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Exudation, as described in this lecture, refers to the movement of what, and to where?","choices":["Leukocytes from the interstitium back into the capillary lumen","Erythrocytes into the alveolar space","Fluid and blood proteins into the interstitial spaces","Fibrin into the vascular lumen to form a thrombus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exudation follows the increased capillary permeability produced by endothelial contraction. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Fibrinous inflammation forms a thick meshwork that can be removed only by what, and what happens if that removal fails?","choices":["Phagocytosis by neutrophils; failure produces pus","Fibrolytic enzymes; failure results in scar tissue formation","Complement fragment C3b; failure results in ulceration","Macrophage digestion; failure results in gangrene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the meshwork can only be removed by fibrolytic enzymes, and that failure of this results in scar tissue formation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is named in the exudate of acute inflammation on the review slide?","choices":["The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The plasma cell","The eosinophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The review describes exudation of fluid, plasma, proteins and leukocytes, with neutrophils named specifically. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Histamine and serotonin share which pair of effects?","choices":["Chemotaxis and opsonisation","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Pain and fibrin deposition","Antibody production and antigen presentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are credited with dilating vessels and raising permeability, which is why they are the earliest vascular mediators. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Granulocytes are characterised under light microscopy by what, and what do those structures primarily do?","choices":["Differently staining cytoplasmic granules that are membrane-bound enzymes acting in the digestion of phagocytized particles","A kidney-shaped nucleus that permits the cell to transmigrate between two adjacent endothelial cells nearby","Azurophilic granules that function purely as antigen-presenting structures","Surface receptors that bind the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The granules are membrane-bound enzymes whose primary action is digesting what has been phagocytized. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which histological findings does the lecture associate with chronic inflammation?","choices":["White blood cells (lymphocytes and macrophages), proliferation of blood vessels, fibrosis and tissue necrosis","Neutrophil predominance with an absence of vascular proliferation","Eosinophil infiltration with mucus hypersecretion","Serous exudate with low protein content, no cellular infiltrate, and no proliferation of blood vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four are listed together as the histological associations of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pattern of inflammation is defined by mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane?","choices":["Serous","Haemorrhagic","Catarrhal","Pseudomembranous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catarrhal inflammation is mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does a monocyte become after leaving the bloodstream, and what does it then do?","choices":["A tissue macrophage that removes dead cell debris and attacks microorganisms","A plasma cell that secretes antibody directly into the interstitium","A fibroblast that lays down scar tissue","A dendritic cell that migrates to the thymus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes monocytes eventually leaving the bloodstream to become tissue macrophages with exactly these roles. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What function does the lecture assign to regulatory (suppressor) T cells?","choices":["Producing antibody directed against antigens the host has encountered previously","Presenting phagocytosed material to naive T cells","Killing tumour cells directly through granule release","Returning the immune system to normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both halves of that role are given, which is why loss of this subset is conceptually linked to autoimmune disease. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Pseudomembranous inflammation consists of a superficial necrotic layer made up of which components?","choices":["Mucus, plasma and erythrocytes","Granulation tissue, proliferating fibroblasts and newly formed capillary networks throughout","Fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris, forming a membrane-like covering over the affected mucosa","Pus alone, without a fibrin component"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That covering over the mucosa is what the term describes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains which combination of material?","choices":["Fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork over the serosal surface","Low-protein plasma with minimal cellular content","A superficial necrotic layer forming a membrane over affected mucosa","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris — that is, pus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the suppurative or purulent pattern as exactly this. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Haemorrhagic inflammation is described as arising from what, and occurring in what relationship to other exudates?","choices":["Mucus hypersecretion, and occurring in isolation","Fibrinogen deposition, and preceding every other exudate pattern","Epithelial sloughing, and replacing other exudates","Damage to blood vessels, and occurring together with other exudates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes haemorrhagic inflammation as damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A patient's inflamed tissue shows a leukocyte infiltrate dominated by cells with bi-lobed nuclei and pink-orange granules. Which process does this pattern most suggest?","choices":["An acute bacterial infection","An allergic or parasitic process","A chronic autoimmune process","A humoral antibody response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bi-lobed nuclei with pink-orange granules identify eosinophils, which predominate in allergic reactions and parasitic infections. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why can a neutrophil kill only a limited number of pathogens before dying?","choices":["It cannot renew its lysosomes, so it dies after phagocytosing a few pathogens","It lacks the enzymes needed to digest the bacterial cell wall it engulfs","It must first be opsonised before it can engulf anything","It converts into a macrophage after a single encounter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inability to renew lysosomes is precisely why the neutrophil is a short-lived, single-use phagocyte. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Inflammation is defined in this lecture as which of the following?","choices":["The reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury","The proliferation of fibroblasts following any cellular insult","The activation of the adaptive immune system by a foreign antigen","The death of tissue through coagulative or liquefactive pathways"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The definition given is specifically the reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury — vascularity is part of the definition, which is why avascular tissue cannot mount one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Vasodilation of the arterioles supplying an injured region produces which immediate effect?","choices":["Reduced hydrostatic pressure within the capillary bed","Contraction of the capillary endothelium","Movement of blood proteins into the interstitium","Increased blood flow to the injured region"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs vasodilation of the supplying arterioles directly with increased blood flow. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which cell types characterise chronic inflammation on review?","choices":["Neutrophils and eosinophils predominantly, without mononuclear infiltrate","Macrophages, monocytes and mononuclear cells including lymphocytes and plasma cells","Basophils and mast cells only","Fibroblasts in the absence of leukocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The review slide lists exactly these, accompanied by proliferation of blood vessels and connective tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the cellular events of acute inflammation after stasis?","choices":["Leukocyte adhesion, transmigration, chemotaxis, aggregation, phagocytosis","Chemotaxis, phagocytosis, adhesion, transmigration, aggregation","Transmigration, adhesion, aggregation, phagocytosis, chemotaxis","Aggregation, chemotaxis, transmigration, adhesion, phagocytosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adhesion to the endothelium precedes movement across it, then directed migration, gathering at the site, and finally ingestion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Basophils are chiefly responsible for which response, and through which mediator?","choices":["Parasitic defence, through major basic protein","Antibody production, through immunoglobulin G","Cell-mediated immunity, through T cell receptors","Allergic and antigen response, through histamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture attributes allergic and antigen response to basophils via histamine, which causes inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What colour do eosinophil granules characteristically assume, and what is the nuclear shape?","choices":["Pale lilac granules, multilobed nucleus","Coarse dark granules, bi- or tri-lobed nucleus","Pink-orange granules, bi-lobed nucleus","No visible granules, kidney-shaped nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cytoplasm is full of granules that assume a characteristic pink-orange colour, with a bi-lobed nucleus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why can an implanted medical device provoke an inflammatory response even when it is sterile?","choices":["Because sterilisation leaves residual endotoxin behind, and that endotoxin then acts as a chemotactic agent","Because the material becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b, and immune cells recognise that coating and attach to it","Because the device physically obstructs lymphatic drainage","Because implantation always causes bacterial contamination of surrounding tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Opsonisation of the biomaterial is what makes it a target regardless of sterility — the lecture gives this explicitly as the clinical application. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the abbreviation PMN refer to in this lecture?","choices":["All granulocytes","Neutrophils only","All mononuclear leucocytes","Plasma cells and memory B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphonuclear leukocyte, abbreviated PMN, is used to refer to all granulocytes rather than to neutrophils alone. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What effects does the lecture attribute to serotonin?","choices":["Pain generation and leukocyte chemotaxis","Opsonisation of biomaterials","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Formation of a fibrous network trapping microorganisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin is given the same pair of vascular effects as histamine, which is why the two are grouped together. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In the five main processes of the inflammatory response, what immediately follows the migration of neutrophils?","choices":["Increased blood flow","Chemotaxis","Increased permeability","Exudation of plasma proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The order runs increased blood flow, increased permeability, migration of neutrophils, chemotaxis, then leucocyte recruitment and activation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish dry from wet gangrene?","choices":["Dry involves fibrinous exudate; wet involves serous exudate","Dry is coagulative necrosis; wet is liquefactive necrosis arising from infection","Dry gangrene occurs on mucosal surfaces; wet gangrene occurs on serosal surfaces","Dry is acute; wet is chronic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gangrenous inflammation is a severe form of necrosis with or without superimposed bacterial infection, dry being coagulative and wet liquefactive from infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two mediators does the lecture group under plasma proteins?","choices":["Bradykinin and complement components","Histamine and serotonin","Immunoglobulin G and C3b","Fibrinogen and fibrolytic enzymes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the plasma proteins heading, alongside the coagulation system. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
